  "title": "Locating the source of a mysterious beep with Claude",
  "content": "<p>Our house has a box room, full of boxes that we still haven't unpacked from our last move. A couple of weeks ago something in that room started <em>intermittently beeping</em>. The beeps were far enough apart that standing in the room and trying to determine the source of the noise was frustrating, and I really didn't want to dig through every one of those boxes. So I had Claude Code build <a href=\"https://github.com/simonw/beep-locator\">Beep Locator</a>, a software system for triangulating the source of a mysterious beep using multiple devices scattered around the room.</p>\n<p>Here's what Beep Locator looks like after it has been running for a while:</p>\n<p><img alt=\"Screenshot of a web app that uses several phones, tablets and laptops as microphones to locate a mystery beeping sound in a room. The left column has an &quot;Add a listener&quot; panel with a QR code and join link, a &quot;Controls&quot; panel with buttons including &quot;Calibrate&quot;, &quot;Test beep&quot; and &quot;It just beeped!&quot; plus a &quot;Listen for beeps&quot; checkbox, and a &quot;Mystery beep&quot; note reading &quot;3345 Hz · 3 confirmed · remembered across sessions&quot;. Below that, a &quot;Devices&quot; list shows five color-coded listeners (Simon iPhone, New iPad, Old iPad, MacBook Pro, Surface Ultra), each marked &quot;✓ mic working · ✓ speaker working&quot;. The main area is a gridded map with a 1 m scale bar that shows the five devices as colored dots and earlier sounds as small ti",
